    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d437">

                <pb facs="0446" n="348" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg348"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. White to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1900-09-11"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">September 11,
                    1900</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. White, chargé d’affaires, refers to the Department’s instructions of the
                        29th,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d437-f1"/> and reports that he is advised that
                    the British ambassador at St. Petersburg is instructed to acquaint the Russian
                    foreign office that Her Majesty’s Government have received communications from
                    their officers in the Far East in a somewhat imperfect form, and, without
                    forecasting the course which it will be expedient to pursue in the future, they
                    are of opinion that the time has not yet arrived when it will be expedient to
                    withdraw British forces from Pekin.)</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
